From 65bd34a31eb8f1f4369ef6761726f37d9ca204e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 05:00:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat: --arifi-profile and a CPU-only VNNI-repack startup
 advisory

GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK ships OFF because it was measured OFF-is-right on
this rig, where weights are GPU-offloaded and ON costs 77-88% of prompt
throughput. On a CPU-only path the same toggle is the largest win in the fork
(+326.7%/+441.8% prompt). That is a DISCLOSURE gap, not a wrong default, and
these two mechanisms close it without moving anything.

common/arifi-profile.{h,cpp} + --arifi-profile NAME (env LLAMA_ARG_ARIFI_PROFILE
via the existing set_env path). cpu-only and gpu-offload each resolve to a
documented set of RUNTIME env toggles and log every one with its evidence. A
variable the caller already set always wins and is logged as KEPT. An unknown
name is a hard error listing the ones that exist. gpu-offload changes no
behaviour at all - GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK=0 and unset take the same branch - it
exists so a benchmark log states what was in effect. Applied during argument
parsing because the toggles latch into function-local statics at first buffer
init.

The profile lives in common/ and only sets variables the engine already reads,
so ggml gains no dependency on anything and there is nothing extra to carry
across a rebase.

Build-time options are never faked. GGML_ARIFI_ROCMFPX_FORMATS is a CMake
option whose vec_dot is chosen in type_traits_cpu[] at link time, so profiles
REPORT it (compiled ON/OFF, NOT settable here) and never set it, and setting it
in the environment now produces a warning that it is being ignored - with or
without a profile, since the trap does not need one.

src/llama.cpp gains the advisory. It fires only when device selection ended with
zero offload devices, extra bufts are enabled, the model carries Q1_0/Q2_0
tensors, and the caller has not decided. The predicate is deliberately not "is a
GPU present": that question cannot be answered before buffer-type selection, and
what matters is whether THESE tensors offload. Q2_0_G128 does not count - the
loader has remapped it and every repack kernel is hard-wired to 64-value blocks,
so advising it would be false. The no_alloc estimation pass llama-server runs
before the real load is skipped, so it prints once.

Auto-detection remains NOT implemented, on purpose; docs/HARDWARE-PROFILES.md
now says what shipped and lists what did not.

Also corrected in that doc: the CPU-only section claimed --no-host is required.
make_cpu_buft_list() only inserts a host buffer type by iterating the SELECTED
offload devices, so with none selected there is nothing to outrank the CPU extra
buffer types. --no-host is required in the mixed case (-ngl 0 with a device
selected), which is the condition the measurements were taken in.

Origin: ArifiLabs/lane-110C
Evidence: research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/run-journal.jsonl (provenance-and-profiles)
Measured-effect: FUNCTIONAL, NOT TIMED - no throughput number was produced or claimed by this work, and no shipped default moved. Verified on build-vulkan (arifi_sync build exit 0, recipe diff PASS before and after) with qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-Q2_0-g64.gguf: -dev none --arifi-profile cpu-only puts 95.98 MiB into CPU_REPACK and drops the rejected-tensor count from 290 to 122, where the same run without the profile leaves CPU_REPACK at 0.00 MiB. The advisory prints exactly one block on the CPU-only path (168 Q1_0/Q2_0 tensors) and is silent on the Vulkan path, when GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK is already set, and on the no_alloc pass. A run with no new flags produces no new output at all.
